From: Christopher Stenger <chris.h.stenger@gmail.com>
Subject: [PATCH] media: cedrus: add VP9 decoding on the H713

The H713's VE decodes VP9 in hardware. Stock Android does it on every VP9
file -- the VE interrupt ticks once per frame and the vendor's
libawvp9HwAL.so drives it -- but nothing about the interface is documented,
and this driver carried no VP9 at all.

VP9 is the H.265 engine running in H.265 mode, in its own register block at
VE+0x500: control, trigger, status and bitstream registers keep their H.265
meaning, and the rest of the block takes a VP9 one. The interface was
recovered from the vendor library, whose register shadows are exported
under names that carry their offsets (vp9_func_ctrl_reg30, ...). The
bitfield packing, the hardware probability-buffer layout and the symbol
count layout were then mapped by running the library's own routines under
emulation and perturbing one input at a time; each map accounts for every
byte. docs/reference/vp9-ve-register-interface.md has the whole of it.

The engine parses no header. It takes the tile data only (the bitstream
offset skips both headers), all probabilities through one DMA buffer that
also carries the tile table and the segment map, and it writes the symbol
counts back into that buffer. Forward updates, backward adaptation and the
four frame contexts are therefore the CPU's, through the v4l2-vp9 helpers
exactly as rkvdec uses them. This driver completes jobs from the hard
interrupt, so a frame's adaptation runs at the next frame's setup.

Two facts that are not in any vendor register name, both found on hardware:

 - Golden's y-scale is at [31:16] of its scale1 register, where the other
   five scale fields are at [20:5]. The vendor writes it there; putting it
   where the others go corrupts every golden-predicted block even at 1:1,
   from the first frame whose golden differs from its last.

 - The engine writes no motion vectors for an intra frame. The co-located
   MV buffer is one per stream, read and rewritten in place, so after a
   mid-stream key frame it still holds the frame before it and the next
   frame predicts from stale vectors. Zeroing it on intra frames gives every
   key frame the state the stream's first one gets.

Profile 0, 8-bit 4:2:0 only; 10-bit (profile 2) has its own registers and
is left out. The capability is added on the H713 alone -- the H6, whose
compatible the H713 VE binds, has no VP9 on its VE.

Tested bit-exact against libvpx (PSNR inf, every frame, VE interrupts =
frames decoded) through GStreamer's v4l2slvp9dec: key frame alone; one tile
and four; 352x288 to 1920x1080 and an odd 350x286; libvpx defaults (hidden
alt-ref frames, compound prediction, backward adaptation, previous-frame
MVs); frame-parallel; error-resilient; segmentation with aq-mode 1 and 3;
lossless; and real content with a mid-stream key frame. Scaled references
are programmed but untested: GStreamer drops frames on a non-key-frame
resolution change, so it cannot feed that path.

Known limit: buffer addresses are taken as addr >> 10 << 2, so the capture
chroma plane must be 1 KiB aligned; that fails only when both
bytesperline / 32 and the 16-aligned height / 16 are odd.

Origin: https://github.com/cstenger/allwinner-h713-mainline/blob/43b2cdd4aa1b161a281db8b10f02ce70085e2d42/patches/kernel/0143-media-cedrus-add-h713-vp9-decoding.patch (0143-media-cedrus-add-h713-vp9-decoding.patch, commit fb1a239)
---
